  "final_text": "I finished all four steps and checked each change by reading it back from Kibana.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) sits on the index `kibana_sample_data_flights`. Its time field is **`timestamp`**. The other fields are:\n   - **Numbers:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightTimeMin`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n   - **True/false:** `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`\n   - **Locations:** `OriginLocation`, `DestLocation`\n   - **Text:** `Carrier`, `FlightNum`, `FlightTimeHour`, `FlightDelayType`, and the origin and destination details (airport ID, city, country, region, weather)\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-85157af7 Flights' (id `bf97e5b0-b885-41e6-b414-0a4ea61151b6`) has exactly two panels on that data view, and its default time range is the last 7 days:\n   - a line chart of flight count over time\n   - a bar chart of flight count for the top 10 carriers\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-85157af7 Cancelled flights' (id `29d326fc-dab0-4e3f-a948-95aa4a6398b2`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on the flights data view. It runs every minute, counts flights matching `Cancelled : true` over the last 5 minutes, and fires when the count is above 10. It was created already enabled, so I didn't need to turn it on.\n4. **Edit and check:** I renamed the carrier panel to 'Flights by carrier', and reading the dashboard back shows the new title. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`, and its last run succeeded (status `ok`).\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
